---
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import BankingProjectVisual from "../../components/BankingProjectVisual.astro";
import { mobileBankingTech } from "../../data/portfolio";
import PortfolioLayout from "../../layouts/PortfolioLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../utils/paths";
---

<PortfolioLayout title="Htet Lin Maung | Mobile Banking" currentPath="/projects">
	<section class="page-banner">
		<div class="container project-detail-hero banking-detail-hero">
			<div class="project-detail-copy">
				<p class="eyebrow">Project Detail</p>
				<h1>Mobile Banking</h1>
				<p>
					A Flutter Mobile Banking application and Angular iBanking web application using the same
					serverless AWS backend services.
				</p>

				<div class="banking-confidential-note">
					<Icon name="bankLock" />
					<p>
						This is a company project. Source code, repository access, and the live system cannot
						be shared publicly.
					</p>
				</div>

				<ul class="chip-list project-detail-stack">
					{mobileBankingTech.map((technology) => <li>{technology}</li>)}
				</ul>

				<div class="project-detail-actions">
					<a class="button primary" href={withBase("/projects")}>
						Back to Projects
						<Icon name="arrow" />
					</a>
				</div>
			</div>

			<BankingProjectVisual />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container project-detail-section">
			<div class="project-detail-story">
				<div class="project-detail-story-intro">
					<p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
					<h2 class="section-title">A modern banking platform for mobile and web.</h2>
						<p>
							The platform separates client delivery, general banking APIs, transaction processing,
							transactional writes, account-balance reads, and private core-banking connectivity.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="project-detail-story-copy">
					<article class="project-detail-narrative">
						<span>01</span>
						<h3>The platform</h3>
						<p>
							Customers use the Flutter application for Mobile Banking and the Angular application
							for iBanking. The Angular iBanking application is hosted as a static web application
							on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and delivered through Amazon
							CloudFront. Login, logout, and user-management requests pass through Amazon API
							Gateway to the General API AWS Lambda function, which calls Amazon Cognito
							internally. An API Gateway authorizer validates tokens before protected requests
							reach the backend.
						</p>
					</article>

					<article class="project-detail-narrative">
						<span>02</span>
						<h3>My responsibility</h3>
						<p>
							I led the development of the mobile banking platform, coordinating the client and
							backend implementation around shared banking workflows. The backend separates
							general API handling from transaction-related endpoints to keep responsibilities
							clear.
						</p>
					</article>

					<article class="project-detail-narrative">
						<span>03</span>
						<h3>Architecture evolution</h3>
						<p>
							I independently designed the earlier company architecture using Azure Functions and
							a custom identity and access management (IAM) microservice running in a container
							on an Azure Virtual Machine, with MongoDB for identity-related data. That design
							reflected my cloud architecture experience at the time. After gaining deeper
							experience, I independently redesigned the architecture around AWS Lambda, Amazon
							Cognito, managed data services, and clearer workload boundaries.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section project-detail-architecture-section banking-architecture-section">
		<div class="container project-detail-section">
			<div class="section-head project-detail-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
				<h2 class="section-title">Two client channels, serverless services, one private banking connection.</h2>
				<p class="project-detail-architecture-context">
					I independently redesigned this architecture based on the lessons I learned after designing
					and delivering the original company system. It represents the main system flow, not the
					company&apos;s exact production architecture. The AWS resources will be defined and
					deployed as infrastructure as code using AWS CDK with TypeScript.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-architecture">
				<div class="project-detail-architecture-card banking-architecture-card">
					<a
						class="banking-architecture-image-link"
						href={withBase("/projects/mobile-banking-architecture.png?v=20260728-8")}
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener"
						aria-label="Open the Mobile Banking architecture diagram at full size"
					>
						<img
							class="project-detail-architecture-image banking-architecture-image"
							src={withBase("/projects/mobile-banking-architecture.png?v=20260728-8")}
							width="2464"
							height="1360"
							loading="lazy"
							decoding="async"
							alt="Architecture for Flutter Mobile Banking and Angular iBanking using Amazon Cognito, Amazon API Gateway, AWS Lambda, Amazon RDS Proxy, Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon SQS FIFO, Amazon SNS transactional SMS, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon CloudFront, Amazon S3, and AWS Site-to-Site VPN"
						/>
					</a>
				</div>

				<div class="project-detail-architecture-notes banking-architecture-notes">
					<article>
						<span>01</span>
						<h3>Mobile and web delivery</h3>
						<p>
							Mobile Banking calls Amazon API Gateway directly. The Angular iBanking application
							is hosted as a static web application on Amazon S3 and delivered through Amazon
							CloudFront. Both clients use the same API Gateway and Lambda backend. The General
							API Lambda calls Amazon Cognito internally for login, logout, and user management,
							while an API Gateway authorizer validates tokens for protected requests.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article>
						<span>02</span>
						<h3>Separated AWS Lambda paths</h3>
						<p>
							General banking endpoints and transaction-related endpoints run through separate
							AWS Lambda functions. Both connect through Amazon RDS Proxy to Amazon Aurora
							Serverless PostgreSQL. The General API uses a smaller Lambda configuration for
							relational user data, limit configuration, and other application settings. The
							transaction API uses a larger Lambda configuration for heavier transactional
							processing. Ordered transaction events use an event-sourcing flow through Amazon SQS
							FIFO and a synchronization Lambda function, which updates the Amazon DynamoDB read
							model used for account-balance queries. After successful processing, the transaction
							Lambda publishes a transactional SMS notification through Amazon SNS.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article>
						<span>03</span>
						<h3>Core banking integration</h3>
						<p>
							The transaction-processing AWS Lambda function communicates with the core banking
							API through an AWS Site-to-Site VPN rather than exposing the core system publicly.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container project-detail-section">
			<div class="section-head project-detail-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Engineering Focus</p>
				<h2 class="section-title">Secure APIs, managed authentication, and efficient account-data access.</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-principles">
				<article>
					<h3>Shared API layer</h3>
					<p>
						Mobile Banking and iBanking use one Amazon API Gateway entry point and the same AWS
						Lambda backend. The General API manages user data, limit configuration, and shared
						banking settings consistently across Flutter and Angular.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<h3>Managed authentication</h3>
					<p>
						Login, logout, and user-management requests reach Amazon Cognito through the General API
						Lambda rather than directly from either client. An Amazon API Gateway authorizer
						validates tokens and guards protected endpoints.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<h3>CQRS read model</h3>
					<p>
						Transactional writes and balance queries use separate data models. Ordered events from
						the event-sourcing flow pass through Amazon SQS FIFO and synchronize account-balance
						data into Amazon DynamoDB for fast reads.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-closing banking-project-closing">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">Company Project</p>
					<h2>Architecture overview only. Source code and production access remain private.</h2>
				</div>
				<a class="button secondary" href={withBase("/projects")}>
					Back to Projects
					<Icon name="arrow" />
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</PortfolioLayout>
